  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nAt 2 PM, a cold front was evident on Doppler radar stretching \nfrom just east of St. Cloud south to Hutchinson and just west of\nNew Ulm. Temperatures ahead of the front have risen into the mid\n70s with a sharp drop into the 40s and 50s behind it. Clouds \nand showers have been persistent across western MN all day and \nare not showing signs of expanding eastward yet. The showers \nappear tied to a nearly stationary pwat gradient despite a \nprogressive surface cold front. Eventually drier air aloft will \nspread southeast and focus shower activity farther south and \neast this evening. Due to ridging across the MS Valley, the \n1-1.25 inch pwat plume won't clear the region and hang up \nacross southern MN. This will keep low chances for showers \ngoing across southern/eastern MN and WI through Sunday morning. \n\nAn upper low over the Desert Southwest today will advance to \nthe Rockies late Sunday. As it approaches, shower activity will\nincrease across the central Plains to southern and western MN by\nSunday night. The low will track from central NE to eastern SD\nto northern MN Monday into Tuesday. The best moisture transport\nwill be confined to western MN and is where the best chances for\na soaking rain is forecast. Rainfall totals of around an inch \nare likely across far western MN with amounts tapering off to \nthe east. The system will exit to the north early Tuesday.\nAnother disturbance will quickly follow Tuesday night and\nWednesday, bringing the next chance for showers. \n\nA ridge will remain parked over the Appalachians and Ohio \nValley through the rest of the forecast period. Southwest flow\nwill bring another several disturbances northeast across the\nUpper Midwest, all bringing with them chances for precipitation.\nPredictability of each remains low and so PoPs are currently\nlittering the forecast. As predictability increases over the\nnext couple days we should be able to fine tune PoPs a bit\nbetter. Regardless of specific timing, it's looking like an\nactive period ahead. \n\nTemperatures will be cool to start the week, but then will warm\nback to the 60s and quite possibly 70s as the week wears on with\nbroad southerly flow developing in advance of a larger scale \nsystem late week.",
